INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6598,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12217288','Development and validation of an ethical monopoly board game to teach bioethics to undergraduate medical students.','Fatima A, Mahboob U, Khan RA, Rafique S.','2025','rampant lahore aleem colourful defendable lodged softer psychometrically lahore aleem lahore educationalists lahore aleem psychometrically phasic methodically catalogued lahore aleem lahore aleem demonstrators scribe briefed otter familiarization transcriptions rechecked sjts snowballing educationist pretesting lahore sjts aleem aloud otter lahore lahore demonstrators step1 step1 fgd sjts annexure colorful quests badges dereliction dishonesty dynamism scared grab educationalists colorful enterprising sjts quests sjts softer sjts sjts sjts relatable inspirational badges pleasantly multiplayer cultivates essentiality catching grasping divisive sjts adventure playful zest playful fines relatable enthusiastically colorful educationalists aleem ea24 lahore briefed copyrights iserson sawicki indystar darrel markose bioallied barman1 sarma nath jfmpc spage epage aulast faizi madan patley nagpal litigations eckles meslin helft zakaria fatimi maqbool fatimi ashfaq ishaq shahzad jfmpc spage epage aulast ashfaq ateeb zakki altaf junaid haripur e083521 khatri hester merrick bowl shidler poel zakaria saripan subarimaniam ethoshunt christen faller gotz icst macer drj unescobkk rushap schrier bul kcm dunwell lameras liebert tannahill olgers bouma maaten s13089 brocklehurst katsarov andorno eichinger schmocker christen hoice katsarov umed mogali sneath tsoy rempel mercuri pardhan p073 gridlocked s82 schifferdecker hammick dornan grafton gurbutt patricio lentz inclusiveness cheryl lentz leadersh dutra echevarria guanilo sparapani lanzoni schoolchildren e20201107 norberta texto ayre scally lawshe koch1977_agreement hubley artino rochelle dezee gehlbach zibarras zibarras coan kerrin pisano santibanez osorio bloody maddineshat yousefzadeh mohseni maghsoudi ghaffari deater deckard mmtr plass kinzer sadhu defoor curric eml henriques acpt validacao jogo educativo sexualidade adolescentes pesqui cuid arcs instr catrib amf lpcm branco dutra echevarria guanilo sparapani lanzoni schoolchildren msn viana e20190083 tnb tmm acds rxe texto tce 5jd98csbgc35zcycyjhnrpm gazzinelli rng rmgs montrezor raskurazhev kuznetsova khizhnikova klochkov bakulin annushkin neuropoly inal gameplay univers s10209','Abstract Background Ineffective teaching practices of bioethics are responsible for the rampant incidents of litigation, reports of misconduct, and complaints of unethical behaviors among medical professionals in various healthcare settings. There is a limited body of research on the implementation of innovative approaches for teaching ethical reasoning. One such approach is game-based learning, with board games emerging as a cost-effective and accessible tool for bioethics education. Aim To develop a board game to improve the teaching of bioethics and evaluate the effects of this board game on student satisfaction, engagement, and knowledge gain regarding bioethics. Methods The board game ‘Ethical Monopoly’ was developed and validated using a mixed-methods study design, following the steps of AMEE Guide 87. This study was conducted at the University of Lahore and Al-Aleem Medical College, from 2021–2023. The game’s content, design, and mechanics were developed using the combined results of a literature review and four focus group discussions with 16 undergraduate medical students and 11 faculty members. Situational Judgment tests were designed to familiarize the players with ethical dilemmas. A panel of 16 multidisciplinary expert judges evaluated the content validity of the Ethical Monopoly in a single round of the Delphi technique. The response process validity of the game was measured through observation and cognitive interviews with a group of eight undergraduate medical students. Results The Ethical Monopoly game was validated using a data collection instrument comprising 55 items on Game Rules (15 items), Game Design (four items), Game Cards (12 items), Game Relevance and Satisfaction (24 items). The content validity index, Scale-Level Content Validity Index Average (S-CVI/Ave) for board games was 0.93. Qualitative analysis of the cognitive interviews showed excellent response process validity. Students appreciated the user-friendly and colourful game design and the use of diverse and realistic scenarios in Situational Judgment tests. Conclusion Expert judges positively received an ethical monopoly prototype. It is a valid board game for teaching ethics with relevant learning objectives, attractive design, and game mechanics that are suitable for improving student motivation, interaction, and interest in learning bioethics. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-025-07454-7. Keywords: Bioethics, Experiential learning, Medical Ethics, Undergraduate medical education, Validation studies Background Bioethics is a branch of applied ethics that applies ethical principles and decision making to solve actual or anticipated dilemmas in medicine and biology. Ethics involves the use of consistent and defendable critical reasoning to resolve complicated moral problems [ 1 ] Teaching medical ethics is an important aspect of an undergraduate medical curriculum. Bioethics is a fundamental component of medical practice, as medical treatments involve vulnerable subjects and often invade patients'' bodies. Serious ethical concerns are raised over respect for patient autonomy, informed consent, confidentiality, patient privacy, research ethics, medical negligence, and many other similar issues [ 2 – 5 ]. The American Association of Medical Colleges and the General Medical Council emphasize the mandatory integration of bioethics into medical curricula. An essential component of the ethics education is ethical reasoning, a complex skill that is difficult to teach using traditional instructional methods employed in medical schools. The teaching of bioethics is heavily hindered by the abstract concepts and theoretical aspects of ethics, lack of time and limited faculty resources, large student-to-teacher ratio in large group teaching/lectures, lack of small-group teaching strategies, poor staff development, insufficient integration of bioethics with other disciplines, absence of practicality and clinical relevance, and improper assessment [ 6 ]. The situation is particularly grave in developing countries, such as Pakistan, where most medical schools lack dedicated and effective courses on medical and research ethics [ 7 , 8 ].Most undergraduate and postgraduate medical students in Pakistan remain unaware of the codes of ethical conduct, common penalties, or the resolution of complex ethical dilemmas, as outlined by regulatory body of the Pakistan Medical & Dental Council (PM&DC) [ 9 , 10 ]. The consequences of inadequate ethics education are evident by the ever-growing rates of litigation, media reports of misconduct, and complaints of unethical behaviors lodged against medical professionals in various healthcare settings. Medical curricula have traditionally employed lectures, role-plays, small-group discussions, and debates etc. to teach medical ethics. However, these teaching methods still lack effectiveness because ethical studies are more demanding for the students due to the requirement of deep self-reflection and critical analysis [ 11 ]. Moreover, little work has been done on designing or employing innovation in teaching ethical reasoning. Game-based learning is one such innovative teaching strategy, among many others. Games are an efficient tool for presenting complex problems in a fun, stimulating, and appealing manner [ 12 ]. Literature widely suggests that educational games enhance student motivation, engagement, critical thinking, communication skills and team work and are a reliable tool for teaching of softer skills [ 13 ]. Game-based learning is proposed for teaching ethical reasoning in many disciplines [ 14 , 15 ]. The use of classroom games [ 16 – 18 ] and video games has been proposed for teaching medical ethics [ 19 ] to undergraduate students, however, there is a lack of empirical data evaluating their effectiveness through pilot studies. Among available game formats, the use of board games has always gained widespread recognition as effective, user friendly and low-cost educational tools [ 20 ]. Board games have been have been widely used to adapt novel pedagogical strategies in various medical specialties [ 21 ]. Educators have used board games for teaching basic sciences and clinical skills in medical and dental education [ 22 – 26 ]. However, according to our literature search, no intervention has utilized a psychometrically robust board game to teach bioethics in undergraduate medical education. To address this gap in teaching of medical ethics, we propose a novel teaching method: the use of a board game to teach bioethics to undergraduate medical students. Our objectives are to develop and validate a board game for improving student engagement and motivation in teaching bioethics, and to evaluate the effects of this board game on student satisfaction, engagement and knowledge gain regarding bioethics. Materials and methods This study was a multicenter project involving the undergraduate medical students and teaching faculty members from the two private medical institutes of Pakistan, the University of Lahore and Al-Aleem Medical College, Lahore. The expert panel of judges included medical educationalists, bioethicist experts, and educational game experts from Pakistan, Iran, and Egypt. The study was completed in one year after obtaining Ethical Approval from the University of Lahore and Al-Aleem Medical College. A detailed description about research was provided to all the participants and their consent was obtained. We used an instrument development, multiphase, mixed-method study design [ 27 ] based on the first six steps of AMEE(The International Association for Health Professions Education) guide No.87 for the development and validation of this educational game. The guide was selected as it provides a rigorous framework for developing and validating a psychometrically sound educational instrument. The game was based on the famous board game monopoly. Monopoly was chosen because of popularity, ease of use and simplicity of rules. The study comprised of two phases (Fig. 1 ): Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Mixed Method, Multi-phasic Development and Validation of ‘Ethical Monopoly’ Phase 1 involved Steps 1–4 of AMEE guide including conduction of Literature review, conduction of Focus group discussions (FGDs), combination of results from literature review and FGDs and development of board game ‘Ethical Monopoly’. The prototype of board game ethical monopoly is shown in Fig. 2 . Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Ethical Monopoly—Game Board Phase 2 included steps 5 & 6 of AMEE guide including Expert Content validation of board game and conduction of cognitive interviews for estimation of Response process validity. Phase 1: ‘Ethical monopoly’ game development Step 1 literature review The first step in phase 1 included a comprehensive systematic review of the literature using PubMed, ERIC databases and Google Scholar search engine. This literature review was conducted based on Best Evidence Medical Education, BEME Guide 13 [ 28 ] and Twelve tips for undertaking a focused systematic review in medical education. [ 29 ]. The objectives of the literature reviews were: To review the existing literature on the use of games in teaching bioethics to undergraduate medical students. To identify and review the existing literature on Development and validation of an educational game. To explore the key elements (design and mechanics) of an educational game. To explore the common bioethical issues reported in literature. The literature search was started through identification of key words and MeSH words. Later on, these key terms and MeSH words were methodically typed in various search engines and databases to retrieve relevant results. The selected studies were catalogued in the library of a referencing software. The results of literature review were condensed into sub-themes and themes through manual data analysis. Step 2 Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) In Step 2, four focus group discussions were organized with faculty members and undergraduate medical students. Based on the literature review, eight questions were developed to analyze the perception of undergraduate medical students and medical faculty regarding the use of game-based learning, perception of bioethics and its teaching, and identifying themes and key elements to be included in an educational game. These questions were piloted in an interview before the conduction of FGDs. Four separate FGDs involved 11 faculty members, six from the University of Lahore and five from the Al-Aleem Medical College. Among the 16 MBBS students, eight were from the University of Lahore and eight were from Al-Aleem Medical College. Maximum variation, purposive sampling was used to recruit faculty members at varying levels of professional hierarchy (Professors, Associate and Assistant Professors and Senior Demonstrators) from various basic and clinical science departments for obtaining diverse opinions in FGDs. The study only included faculty members with a minimal teaching experience of two years to ensure authenticity in discussions. Similarly, students from years 4 and 5 of MBBS program were invited because of the maximal clinical exposure and patient interaction during their clinical rotations and clinical clerkships. The participants were invited for face-to-face discussion. All four FGDs were conducted, as per the convenience of the participants, at campuses of the respective institutes from February to 2021-March 2021. A research team was established to help with the focus groups. The first author took responsibility for the moderator, whereas the fourth author was assigned the role of a scribe and for time management. Consent was obtained from participants after they were briefed about the project. Discussion among group participants was generated based on open-ended questions about game-based learning and the teaching of bioethics. FGDs were stopped after data saturation was reached. Data was collected by audio-recording the discussions . The audio recording was transcribed using Otter.ai and manual transcription. Analysis of the transcribed data was done manually by color coding in a Microsoft Word worksheet. Some additional aid was taken from NVivo Qualitative data management software after familiarization with transcripts, and a thematic analysis was performed. During thematic data analysis, initially, ‘open codes’ were identified from transcriptions and then a constant comparative method was used for axial coding and generation of sub-themes. Results were checked twice using triangulation. Statements were rechecked by the authors and study participants (member check) for its validation and accuracy. It established the consistency of results. Step 3 and 4: data synthesis and game development In step 3, a comprehensive list of sub-themes regarding the game content and game elements was created by merging the results of the literature review and focus groups. Based on the recommendations obtained through Steps 1–3, a prototype of a board game for teaching bioethics was designed in Step 4 (Fig. 2 ). The game content was arranged in the form of Situational Judgment Tests (SJTs) which were developed by a team of undergraduate medical faculty facilitated by the first and fourth author. Ethical monopoly prototype consisted of board game, game cards (Reward Cards, Question card, Penalty Card) and ethical monopoly money. Phase 2: “Ethical monopoly” validation Step 5 modified delphi technique for expert validation of board game Content validity of the board game was determined using a single round of a modified Delphi technique for content validation [ 30 , 31 ]. To validate the board game content, a panel of 24 multi-disciplinary experts was selected using purposive and snowballing sampling techniques [ 32 , 33 ] from Pakistan, Iran, and Egypt. A 55-item questionnaire regarding four categories related to the board game objectives, including items for validation of game rules [ 15 ], game design validation [ 4 ], game cards validation [ 12 ] and game relevance and satisfaction [ 24 ] was shared through email. The experts in our study were selected based on the following inclusion criteria: i. Bioethicists having a postgraduate degree/certification in the discipline of Bioethics or have at least two years of experience in teaching bioethics. ii. Game Developers with experience in developing at least one educational game. iii. Medical Educationist with a postgraduate degree in Medical Education with publications in fields of either bioethics or Game-based learning. In modified Delphi, the experts were invited via WhatsApp and email by sending an invitation text with a link to a Google Form containing a specific data collection instrument, composed of two parts: Part one of the instrument contained information regarding the research title, research objectives, research question, selection criterion of judges, the purpose of the game, instruction on rating items in the second part of the data collection instrument and how this instrument fits in the current study. The second part of the instrument contained informed consent and 55 items regarding the board game. All the relevant documents of the Ethical Monopoly board game (Ethical Monopoly Objectives and Rules, Ethical Monopoly Introduction Video, Ethical Monopoly-Game Board, Question Cards, Question Key, Extra Cards, Ethical Monopoly-Money) were embedded in the Google Form for easy viewing of the Expert Judges. Participants were requested to rate each game item on a 4-point Likert scale ( strongly disagree = 1, disagree = 2, agree = 3, strongly agree = 4 ), and were asked for additional comments for improvement and suggestions regarding any further additions to the game items. After a period of one week, e-mail reminders were sent to the expert judges, and we waited for three weeks, to get their responses. The validation process lasted from November -December 2022. Sixteen out of these twenty-four Expert Judges consented to take part in the study. The expert judge data was analyzed using Microsoft Excel software. Content validity Index (CVI) and the content validity ratio (CVR) are the most important empirical measures for calculating the content validity of any instrument. Content Validity Index is most commonly used statistical measure for validation of any instrument. CVI index comprises of Item-CVI (I-CVI) and the Scale-level-CVI (S-CVI). In our study, Content Validity Indices (I-CVI & S-CVI) [ 20 ] and CVR [ 21 ] were calculated based on the Expert validation scores. For an expert panel comprising of 16 Judges (Ne = 16), the cut-off value for CVR critical was set at 0.500 [ 34 ]. The inter-rater reliability for assessing agreement among multiple expert judges, to factor out chance agreement, was calculated through Fleiss’s kappa and results were interpreted for corresponding ranges [ 35 , 36 ]. The basic formula for Fleiss''s kappa is: κ = (po—pe)/(1—pe) where, po: is the observed proportion of agreement among raters. pe: is the expected proportion of agreement by chance. The values of Fleiss’s Kappa range from −1 to + 1, with values closer to 1 indicating better agreement. Our study only used a single round of Delphi as the game achieved the desired consensus (as indicated by the value of S-CVI) by Experts in the first round. Thus, the authors benefited from the collective wisdom of experts without the need for prolonged iterations. This approach allowed for efficient use of time and resources while still ensuring a comprehensive and well-informed consensus-building activity. Step 6 response process validity using observation and cognitive interviews Response process validity evaluates how prospective respondents understand the items of an instrument. Response process validity of the board game was measured through observation [ 37 ] and cognitive pretesting interviews [ 38 ]. We interviewed eight Final-year MBBS students from the University of Lahore through purposive, non-probability sampling. Final Year MBBS students were selected due to their maximal patient interaction and clinical exposure during clerkships. Moreover, the SJTs were designed as per role analysis of a house officer/junior doctor including role-specific responsibilities, tasks, knowledge, skills and abilities, hence Final Year students were most suited for response process validity calculation. Students of the Al-Aleem Medical College could not be included due to their Final Professional Examination. After obtaining informed consent from the participants, a brief introduction to the research project was given through a printed sheet. All the participants engaged in a 30-min game session. The first author observed the session whereas the fourth author acted as a facilitator. Participants were asked to clarify any confusion regarding the game. After the game session, students were interviewed by using “think aloud” and “verbal probing” techniques using 55 items of the data collection instrument regarding the clarity and relevance of the board game. Students were asked to choose appropriate response answers from pre-defined themes. Students were asked to “strongly agree/agree/disagree” or “Clear/somewhat clear/unclear” to each item in the instrument. In addition to pre-set codes, students were encouraged to share their experience regarding each theme. The data was transcribed using Otter.ai and later interpreted using manual qualitative analysis. The final-year student participants were requested through a faculty member working at the University of Lahore. The first author, herself, does not work or teach in the University of Lahore or any affiliated hospital. Hence, it was ensured that responses were unbiased and not influenced by any external factors. All authors ensured that participant answers remained unbiased and genuine, preserving the integrity of the results. Result The results of phase 1- ‘Ethical Monopoly Game Development’ (step 1–4) are given in Fig. 3 and Table 1 . The sample of 16 undergraduate medical students (4th Year and Final Year MBBS) included 5 male students and 11 female students. The sample of 11 MBBS teaching faculty members included 2 male faculty members and 9 female faculty members. Among these faculty members, there were five Professors (Departments of Anatomy, Pharmacology, Ophthalmology, Biochemistry, and Medical Education), one Associate Professor (Departments of Pathology) five senior demonstrators (Departments of Community Medicine, Physiology, Medical Education, and Behavioral Sciences). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Result of Phase 1(Step1-4): “Ethical Monopoly” Game Development Table 1. Summary of Phase 1 (Step1-4)“Ethical Monopoly” Game Development Results Step 1:Literature Review Themes Step 2:FGD Themes Step 3 and 4: Combined Themes of Step 1 & 2 used in game and SJTs development Game design and game mechanics Defined objectives, Epic meaning Defined Goals Clear Learning objectives and Game Rules Stated for Game (See Annexure) Visual Aesthetic Design Appealing constitution, Colorful Design for Game Board and Game Cards Quests, Challenges, Mindful Challenges Question Cards containing Situational Judgment Tests Points/Achievement badges Loss aversion Rewards Property Cards won by Answering Questions Physical Token of achievements in form of Property cards and money bill Interaction Meaningful Social Interaction, Multiple Team Play Bioethical Dilemmas Components of Ethical Training should include Autonomy, Professional misconduct, Relationship with the pharmaceutical industry Research ethics, Respect for cultural diversity and pluralism., Solidarity and cooperation, Euthanasia Reproductive Ethics HIV and sexually transmitted diseases Using Patients for Education and Training Organ Transplantation Ethics, Conflict of interest, Rights of patients and doctors in International law & Constitution of Pakistan, PM&DC Context-specific Ethical dilemmas to include Illicit Monetary Gains, Dereliction, Poor Communication Dishonesty/Malicious Intent, Malpractice, Violation of human Right Reflected in Question Card Content (Development of Situational Judgment tests) Open in a new tab A total of six themes, regarding educational game design, game mechanics and common bioethical dilemmas were identified from the literature as well as through focus group discussions (Table 1 ). The game was developed (step 3 and 4) based on the combined information obtained from the first two steps (Table 1 ). Phase 1: ‘Ethical monopoly’ game development results Phase 2: ‘Ethical monopoly’ validation results The item-level content validity index (I-CVI) ranged from 0.73 to 1, that is, good to excellent for individual items. The overall content validity index, Scale-Level Content Validity Index Average (S-CVI/Ave) for board games was 0.93 which is considered excellent (Table 2 ). Universal agreement (UA) score and content validity ratio (CVR) for individual items were also calculated. Table 2. Expert Validation Score & Calculation of Content Validity Indices Item No Items I-CVI UA CVR 1 Number of participants is appropriate 0.88 0 0.75 2 The number of cards is adequate 1.00 1 1.00 3 Introduction is clear and easy to understand 1.00 1 1.00 4 The game’s objective is clear and consistent 0.94 0 0.88 5 Card categories are clear and objective 1.00 1 1.00 6 Card categories are consistent and appropriate 1.00 1 1.00 7 Preparation is clear and objective 0.94 0 0.88 8 Preparation is 