 board and cards. But then, I really like the scenarios and I enjoyed playing it” Game design P4: ” Graphics are attractive to grab student’s attention to learn.” Question Cards P7: “This game seems so realistic and related to life and this is important for the normal life experiences. So, this is interesting” Extra Cards P7: “The penalty cards were very interesting. I have observed so many doctors doing the wrong surgeries and giving costly medicines. So, it was interesting to see those cards” Game Relevance and Satisfaction P5: “ This game is very interesting to learn the basic cases and basic scenario of ethics, medical ethics” Structure and Presentation P3: “ We can understand the principles of bioethics like autonomy, normal, non-maleficence or justice. In easiest way possible.” Social Interaction P4: “I liked how we discussed the scenarios with each other. Everyone had a different idea about solution of card. We learnt different perspectives” Relevance P1 : “The scenarios discuss in this game I seem to be very realistic” Open in a new tab Discussion Our study has developed a cost-effective, low-maintenance, and user-friendly educational Board Game (ethical monopoly) for improving student engagement and motivation in teaching of bioethics. In phase 1 of game development, Ethical Monopoly game design and game mechanics were developed using themes emerging from literature review and focus group discussion with undergraduate medical students and faculty. Ethical monopoly contains clear learning objectives and game rules which address the themes of ‘Defined objectives’/‘Epic meaning’/‘Defined Goals’. The game goals are the core concept of a game design, which include teaching objectives and the experience that educationalists want to provide for students. [ 39 ] [ 22 ] The themes of ‘Visual Aesthetic Design’/‘Appealing constitution’ led to creation of colorful and enterprising design for game boards and game cards. Situational Judgment Tests (SJTs) were developed by subject matter experts to create ‘Mindful Challenges’ and ‘Quests’ which are essential elements of educational games. SJTs are generally found to be cost-effective, fair, valid, reliable and well-received tools measuring important non-academic attributes and softer skills (such as empathy, ethical reasoning, communication skills integrity and teamwork), which are central to medical practice [ 40 , 41 ]. Role analysis was done to develop SJTs regarding bioethical dilemmas encountered by young doctors (House officers/post-graduate residents). For improving student motivation, Game cards were designed to have SJTs with varying difficulty Challenges (SJTs), relatable case vignettes, conflict in vignettes, humor, Reward Cards and Punishment Cards and Inspirational Quotes. Similar vignette based games have been shown to prompt medical students and residents to exercise high-value care critical thinking in resolving complex clinical problems. [ 42 – 44 ]. ‘Rewards’/‘Points’/’Achievement Badges’/‘Loss Aversion’ help in achieving flow in any game session. In ethical monopoly, students possess physical tokens of their achievements in the form of Property cards and money bills won by answering questions. In our game, we have developed equal number of Reward and Penalty cards to maintain a balanced state of challenge thus making Ethical Monopoly a ‘pleasantly frustrating’ game for the students [ 45 ]. An Educational Game contains three key elements: a challenge, a response, and feedback. The feedback to student response, in turn, acts as a new challenge and keeps players involved in the game by prompting them to modify their response [ 46 ] Teaching methods that encourage discussion or interaction among the groups are favored by the students as they result in better understanding of the content and enhance their confidence to cope in a learning environment. [ 47 ] ‘Interaction’/‘Meaningful Social Interaction’ is provided in Ethical monopoly by designing a multiplayer game sessions and by introducing facilitator in the role of bank. Social interaction and team work in gaming also improves student performance by stimulating serial testing, allowing for one test’s result to inform the choice of the next. [ 48 ]Enhanced interaction and feedback cultivates experiential learning and long-term retention of knowledge that can be recalled and later applied to complex ethical dilemmas faced during clinical practice. [ 49 ]. In phase 2, The Ethical Monopoly game was validated by a panel of 16 multidisciplinary Expert Judges with an excellent content and design validity index (S-CVI/Ave) of 0.93, reaching 93% validation percentage in a single Delphi round. This result is extremely relevant for educational games because a good educational game should be valid, understandable, and viable. The expert validation instrument used in this study addresses the following four dimensions: A. Game Rules: Game rules include various game steps and desired learning objectives B. Game Design: Game Design involves the graphics and visual details of the game board, game cards, and game interface C. Game Card Content: This domain evaluates the game content for its sufficiency and appropriateness for teaching the desired subject or topic D. Game Satisfaction and Relevance: This measures the significance and relevance of the board game to the desired educational outcome Similarly, designed instruments have been used for the validation of other educational games addressing their content and other game elements, such as photos, game dynamics, and social interaction [ 32 , 50 ]. The content validity of the ethical monopoly is 0.93, which is comparable to that of other game development studies. Validation of game rules Game rules and game mechanics are an essential component of development of Educational Game. For Ethical Monopoly, the Expert judges scored the game with excellent I-CVI ranging between 0.88–1.00, showing relevance and clarity of Game rules in Ethical Monopoly. CVR score for these items ranged between 0.75–1.00 indicating the essentiality of game rules for teaching of relevant construct (bioethics). Comparable values have been reported in similar studies [ 32 ]. Validation of game design and appearance The game design and graphics are important resources for engaging students as well as being essential as resources for visualization of ideas. An eye-catching game design is a great attention grasping strategy for ensuring a state of game flow and improved learning [ 51 ] Regarding design and appearance, the judges scored the game with excellent I-CVI ranging between 0.8–1.00, considering game design to have attractiveness and sufficient clarity. These values were comparable to other surveys for appearance validation of educational games [ 52 ]. Validation of card content For category of Card Content, the expert judges'' scoring yielded I-CVI values ranging between 0.73–1.00. Despite following a rigorous exercise for content development, combining literature review, analyzing bioethical curricula, and multiple focus group discussions, game validation by judges was divisive regarding the bioethical content of the cards. This disparity can be attributed to the subjective nature of ethical reasoning and the complexity of the ethical dilemmas. Another reason for this discordance is the unavailability of a definitive, uniform, and comprehensive syllabus to teach bioethics in Pakistan. Moreover, the current study lacked the resources and manpower to recruit a team of experts on bioethics at the level of bioethical content development. This finding can also be explained based on the diverse nature of expert panels and their varying familiarity with the format of Situational Judgment Tests. It is also worth noting that while designing SJTs, a preference was given to themes and principles emerging from focus group discussions with undergraduate faculty and students, and their understanding of bioethical dilemmas and reasoning might not match the depth of ethical competency demonstrated by expert bioethicists [ 38 ]. The expert judges reported the Card Language to be clear, accessible, appropriate and compatible with the knowledge level of undergraduate medical students, as indicated by I-CVI scores ranging between 0.87–1.00. With easy to understand questions, this finding supports similar values attained by validation of an educational game for teaching first aid to school children [ 53 ], that obtained a satisfactory assessment of clarity, objectivity and attractiveness in two rounds of Delphi, as opposed to a single round in case of Ethical Monopoly. Comparable findings have been reported for language with other validation studies [ 54 ]. Game relevance and satisfaction The Expert Judges showed a disagreement over item 34. The reason for this low agreement may be true for individual players who play the game by using a question key. However, when an ethical monopoly is played in the classroom with a trained facilitator acting as the bank, it provides ample opportunities for students to discuss issues and ambiguities. This assumption is supported by the expert judge''s comments on the item. For “Game Structure and Presentation”, the judges showed good I-CVI values, ranging from 0.80–1.00. Similar values were obtained for another mobile serious game “ Healthy Adventure ” regarding the healthy lifestyle of adolescents [ 55 ]. The most important aspect of any educational game is the attractiveness of its design and the promotion of interpersonal interactions between players. Both these elements ensure that players are engaged until the end of the game session, and thus help improve learning [ 56 ]. As described above, our panel of experts perceived ethical monopoly as both attractive and interactive, making it a useful learning tool. Other important benefit of board games, as reported by similar studies regarding educational board games, include creation of playful game cycle, promotion of social interaction without using any digital or internet tools and easy accessibility [ 57 , 58 ] Just like these board games, Experts agreed that “Ethical Monopoly” also provides both competitive and cooperative mechanics and prompts medical students to engage in ethical education with concentration, skills and an eager zest to learn. Response process validity Response Process validity was evaluated through observation and cognitive interviews. The aim of this step was to ascertain that undergraduate medical students have a similar understanding of game rules, design, and content as the researchers. The literature suggests that the sample size for cognitive interviews must be approximately between ten to 30 participants; however, for smaller studies involving a novel educational strategy, a sample size of five to six participants is sufficient [ 38 ]. The sample size in our study was eight undergraduate medical students (Final Year MBBS). Qualitative analysis of the cognitive interviews showed good Response Process validity. All students manifested a positive attitude towards ethical monopoly and demonstrated a good understanding of the game rules and mechanics. A possible explanation for this positive reception is that all the volunteering participants were already familiar with the game of monopoly, resulting in an improved game performance on this variation of monopoly [ 59 ]. Ethical Monopoly is designed to provide undergraduate medical students with a safe and structured environment for applying bioethical principles to real-world clinical scenarios without real-life implications. The game encourages active engagement and interaction, enabling students to navigate complex ethical concepts in a dynamic and playful setting. A key objective of the game content is to enhance students’ awareness of common legal and ethical challenges in medical practice, including lawsuits, fines, and penalties for unethical conduct. By increasing familiarity with professional codes of ethics, the game aims to reduce instances of unintentional misconduct stemming from a lack of awareness. Additionally, it fosters critical thinking, creativity, and teamwork, equipping students with essential skills for addressing complex ethical dilemmas collaboratively. Ethical Monopoly contributes to the development of communication skills and reflective practice by promoting structured social interaction. Moreover, the game’s adaptable format presents opportunities for broader educational applications. By modifying the Question Cards, the same framework can be utilized to enhance clinical reasoning, counseling, and decision-making skills, extending its utility beyond medical ethics education. This approach highlights the potential of board games as an innovative tool for teaching complex soft skills that are traditionally challenging to impart through conventional methods. Limitations This study was undertaken as a project for masters in medical education program; hence, it lacked the resources and manpower to recruit a team of bioethics experts for the content development of a board game. Moreover, game content may not be relatable and suitable globally for undergraduate medical students because of cultural and religious sensitivity of ethical dilemmas. Expert validation of content validity involved only one round of the Delphi technique. Future recommendations The future researchers should test the effectiveness of the Ethical Monopoly prototype by piloting it with undergraduate medical students and compare the effectiveness of the board game with other teaching methodologies for improvement in ethical knowledge, behavior modification and student satisfaction. The subsequent versions of Ethical Monopoly can be developed with a greater number of cards, with more diverse and comprehensive coverage of ethical dilemmas and ethical conduct for undergraduate medical education. Furthermore, the content of board game can be expanded to include bioethical dilemmas and ethical competencies specified for postgraduate medical students, nursing graduates, and allied health-science students. Conclusion Ethical Monopoly is a novel board game that uses dice, a game board, and game cards (question, reward, and penalty) to improve ethical competence and reasoning of undergraduate medical students. The Ethical Monopoly prototype was enthusiastically received by expert judges with excellent content validity scores, as per values recommended in the literature. The scores indicated that ethical monopoly is a valid board game for teaching ethics with relevant learning objectives, attractive design, and game mechanics that are suitable for improving student motivation, interaction and interest in learning bioethics. The undergraduate medical students demonstrated excellent response process validity for ethical monopoly, with an explicit understanding of game objectives and game rules. Students also appreciated the user-friendly and colorful game design and the use of diverse and realistic scenarios in situational judgment tests. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (20.8MB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors will like to acknowledge medical educationalists, bioethicists and game experts for their timely and proficient feedback on this board game and the medical students and faculty for their active participation in this study. Authors’ contributions A. F. Conceived the study design and methodology. Conducted the primary research and analysis. Wrote and revised the manuscript, and provided critical insights into the interpretation of the data. U. M. Performed Data analysis. Contributed to the discussion and final editing of the manuscript R. A. K. Supported the interpretation of results and revised the manuscript for clarity and coherence. S. R. Contributed to the theoretical framework and literature review. Coordinated the Focus group discussions and developed key figures and tables. Funding This research was not funded by any