 al. A standardized, evidence-based protocol to assess clinical actionability of genetic disorders associated with genomic variation. Genet. Med. 18, 1258–1268 (2016). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Webber EM et al. Evidence-based assessments of clinical actionability in the context of secondary findings: Updates from ClinGen’s Actionability Working Group. Hum. Mutat. 39, 1677–1685 (2018). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Harrison SM & Rehm HL Is ‘likely pathogenic’ really 90% likely? Reclassification data in ClinVar. Genome Med. 11, 72 (2019). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Gallego CJ et al. Penetrance of hemochromatosis in HFE genotypes resulting in p.Cys282Tyr and p.[Cys282Tyr];[His63Asp] in the eMERGE Network. Am. J. Hum. Genet. 97, 512–520 (2015). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Grosse SD, Gurrin LC, Bertalli NA & Allen KJ Clinical penetrance in hereditary hemochromatosis: estimates of the cumulative incidence of severe liver disease among HFE C282Y homozygotes. Genet. Med. 20, 383–389 (2018). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Laberge AM Recommending inclusion of HFE C282Y homozygotes in the ACMG actionable gene list: cop-out or stealth move toward population screening? Genet. Med. 20, 400–402 (2018). [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Roberts AM et al. Integrated allelic, transcriptional, and phenomic dissection of the cardiac effects of titin truncations in health and disease. Sci. Transl. Med. 7, 270ra6 (2015). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Haggerty CM et al. Genomics-first evaluation of heart disease associated with titin-truncating variants. Circulation. 140, 42–54 (2019). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Pujol P. et al. Guidelines for reporting secondary findings of genome sequencing in cancer genes: the SFMPP recommendations. Eur. J. 