INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6648,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12445634','Developing global consensus about core knowledge and skills for genetic counselor education.','McEwen A, Ormond KE, Cathcart-King Y; HUGO Genetic Counselling Education Sub‐Committee; Paneque M.','2025','acgc cagc ebmg hgsa aspirational tagc acgc cbgc ebmg hgsa hgsa acgc cbgc ebmg hgsa ambrosino ashfaq nilakantam aspirational tagc keo pragmatism keeney eubanks marvin mcclaren eth23 tagc novelly keo acgc cbgc ebmg hgsa keeney snape 28th schifano niederberger eubanks advisement immunogenetics electively imprinting electively baty sahhar theorem davys beddoe vercruyssen ingvoldstad tagc tagc tagc keo keo keo tagc eth23 cathcart e70116 milena acgc comptencies acgc brochure_15_web ambrosino tayoun abramowicz zilfalil boughtwood hamdi cendes majumder mascalzoni ndiaye repetto shotelersuk reeder ashfaq kirmani munim naeem furqan unlocking baty cbgc cbgc cccg cowley guilbert agnc eire agnc davys beddoe kingsley eubanks veach callanan ebmg ebmg gcgn_downloads ebmgprofessionalandeducationalstandardsforgeneticcounsellors2021 baiao lemos henriques luxury hgsa hgsa policiesposition ingvoldstad taris e1857 pichert keeney padmanabhan keng keng thong annuar mohd taib marvin allain carmany flodman e70044 mcclaren crellin gaff nisselle davys e70014 resta strecker nilakantam ravi dayananda basavanagowdappa abad nishigaki wessels borensztein hallquist faucett peay tricou uhlmann wain bengoa pasalodos lemos moldovan juhe moldovan juh feroce bjrnevoll els narravula siglen ciuca abuli juhe sequeiros sahhar schifano niederberger voelckel snape kirkham popay britten froggatt lobban jacoby e004217 vercruyssen yashar stalburg marvin e2023','Abstract There are over 130 genetic counselor (GC) training programs documented in more than 30 countries. Some regions also have developed practice‐based competencies and guidelines for accrediting GC training, often including lists of required curriculum items (for example: ACGC, CAGC, EBMG, HGSA). However, areas where genetic counseling is emerging or desired may struggle to create training approaches from these often‐aspirational knowledge and skill development lists. In a collaborative effort between the Human Genome Organization (HUGO) Education Workgroup and the Transnational Alliance for Genetic Counseling (TAGC), we conducted a global consensus process to assess and deliberate over which knowledge and skills were broadly considered ‘core’ to the GC educational process. We utilized an online‐modified Delphi process to survey a purposive global sample of GC education providers and members of credentialing boards from countries with established GC and medical genetics providers in areas where GC education is newly emerging. We identified a list of 64 potential topics through a literature review and review of relevant curriculum guidelines and professional competencies. We then assessed relevance in a 2‐round survey process using the criteria of >80% agreement. Topics that met these criteria, including those that were borderline, were discussed in two online consensus meetings. We present the consensus list of 25 content topics across four major areas of knowledge and skills: counseling and communication, genetics and genomics, medical knowledge, and genetic counseling healthcare professional skills and knowledge that we propose should be included in the GC curriculum for countries where GCs are new or emerging. We also propose 11 additional “Tier 2” topics that could be considered depending on regional needs or as countries evolve the profession. Keywords: curriculum, education, genetic counseling, genetic counselors, knowledge, skills, training What is known about this topic The need for increased access to genetic counseling services is well documented globally. Genetic counselor education and training has been documented in over 30 countries. There are practice‐based knowledge and skills lists and training accreditation guidelines in some, but not all, countries. What this paper adds to the topic Despite extensive lists of professional knowledge and skills required for the accreditation of genetic counselors and the emergence of new training courses in this area, to date there has not been consensus on essential knowledge and skills worldwide. This study identified core knowledge and skills required for students to achieve readiness for practice for entry‐level roles in regions where the profession of genetic counselors is desired or in development. 1. INTRODUCTION The profession of genetic counselors (GCs) started over 50 years ago in the United States (US), with training programs beginning in the United Kingdom (UK), Australia, and South Africa in the 1980s and 1990s. Since then, GCs have begun to practice in at least 45 countries, and GC education has been documented in more than 30 countries across the globe (Ormond et al., 2024 ). The National Society of Genetic Counselors (NSGC) in the US developed a definition of genetic counseling (National Society of Genetic Counselors'' Definition Task Force et al., 2006 ) that has been adopted around the world. Genetic counseling is a communication process focused on facilitating understanding and adaptation to genetic contributions of health conditions. Training and education focus on the knowledge, skills, and attitudes required to provide care that meets this definition of genetic counseling. A recent editorial (Austin, 2024 ) suggested the need to revisit the definition of genetic counseling as the profession expands globally, an exercise that would include ensuring training and education continue to meet the definition. In regions where the profession of GCs is well established, there are practice‐based competencies and guidelines for accrediting GC training (Accreditation Council for Genetic Counseling (ACGC), 2023 ; Canadian Board of Genetic Counselors (CBGC), 2006 ; European Board of Medical Genetics (EBMG), 2021 ; Human Genetics Society of Australasia (HGSA), 2022 ). Accreditation guidelines include formal criteria used to evaluate education and training programs and ensure they meet established benchmarks for quality, safety, and professional competence. In professional practice, competency standards guide what individuals should know, understand, and be able to do to practice effectively and safely. Competency standards provide a foundation for training and certification of GCs and are generally intended for all GCs, regardless of role, specialty area, or practice setting (Human Genetics Society of Australasia (HGSA), 2022 ). Competency standards include knowledge and skills such as verbal and written communication, relationship building with clients and healthcare professionals, education, risk assessment, client‐centered practice, clinical genetics knowledge and skills, case management, advocacy, service delivery, research skills, and ethical and professional practice. Reflective practice, including recognizing skills and areas for development, is a hallmark of GC competency (Accreditation Council for Genetic Counseling (ACGC), 2023 ; Canadian Board of Genetic Counselors (CBGC), 2006 ; European Board of Medical Genetics (EBMG), 2021 ; Human Genetics Society of Australasia (HGSA), 2022 ; Skirton et al., 2010 ). The international literature highlights the need for increased access to genetic counseling education and training to assist with the burgeoning use of genomic medicine globally (Ambrosino et al., 2024 ; Ashfaq et al., 2023 ; Lee et al., 2022 ; Nilakantam et al., 2020 ). The World Health Organization (WHO) recognizes the lack of access to genetic testing and counseling, particularly in low‐ and middle‐income countries (“Accelerating access to genomics for global health: promotion, implementation, collaboration, and ethical, legal, and social issues: a report of the WHO Science Council,” 2006a , “Medical Genetic Services in Developing Countries,” 2006b ). The Human Genome Organization (HUGO) is also working to enhance access to genomic healthcare in low‐ and middle‐income countries. Both the WHO and HUGO recognize the role education and training play. However, areas where the profession of GCs is emerging, or desired, may struggle to create training approaches from the often‐aspirational knowledge and skill development lists, predominantly developed in western countries. As part of a HUGO Genetic Counselling Education workgroup and in collaboration with the Transnational Alliance for Genetic Counseling (TAGC; an international group of genetic counseling educators), this group of researchers (AM, KEO, MP, who themselves have experience in genetic GC training and accreditation guidance development in North America, Europe, and Australasia) began developing a draft list of core knowledge and skills to be used by countries working to establish GC training approaches. In doing so, we realized the power that would occur from having a global consensus process to assess and deliberate over which knowledge and skills were broadly considered ‘core’ to the educational process of GCs. Global consensus has the potential to facilitate formal or informal reciprocal recognition of GC qualifications between countries. We aimed to identify areas of consensus and conflict among international experts regarding the core curricula for GC training in areas where the profession is new or emerging. The overarching goal of the current project was to develop a core set of knowledge and skills that should be considered when establishing training and accreditation guidelines in regions where GCs are an emerging profession. 2. METHODS Since our desired outcome was to develop international guidance on core curriculum components for GC education and training, an online‐modified Delphi provided the ideal opportunity to include relevant stakeholders from around the world. A Delphi is a structured, multi‐round, iterative survey, grounded in pragmatism, and used to facilitate group consensus from experts on a particular topic (Keeney et al., 2001 ; McKenna, 1994 ). This method has previously been used in genetic counseling and genomics research to attain a consensus (Eubanks Higgins et al., 2013 ; Jacobs et al., 2017 ; Marvin et al., 2025 ; McClaren et al., 2020 ; Ormond et al., 2021 ; Paneque et al., 2015 ). In this study, we used a modified Delphi approach, which begins with a structured survey (Chia‐Chien & Brian, 2007 ). Participant experts anonymously responded, and the study team used the responses to design a second‐round survey that builds on the previous round findings. In line with typical Delphi methodology, after round one of data collection, an interim analysis occurred, and the second questionnaire was developed based on the interim results. A summary of round one responses was included with the round two survey, including a heat map to help participants visualize the round one findings. Finally, we held online consensus meetings to discuss the proposed knowledge and skills, timed to offer access to participants globally. The consensus meetings served as part of the initial dissemination and fostered international connections. All study methods and measures underwent Human Research Ethics Committee review by the University of Technology Sydney (UTS), review number ETH23‐8497. 2.1. Participants and recruitment We recruited international GC education providers and genetic counseling education experts/advocates who represent different stages of development of the profession, including: Representatives of areas where the profession and education programs are well developed, including Australasia, Canada, South Africa, UK and US. We purposively recruited experienced genetic GC education providers (training program faculty) and those who have participated on accreditation or certification/registration boards, with expertise in learning design and educational pedagogy Representatives of countries where the GC profession and/or education programs have developed in recent years, for example some parts of Asia and Europe Representatives of areas where the GC profession and/or education programs are not yet developed but are desired and/or in development, for example, some parts of Asia, Central and South America (e.g., Mexico), and Africa (e.g., Ghana). We used purposive sampling to recruit participants who represented a diverse range of countries and stages of development of GC education and practice. We limited representation to no more than four participants per country. GC education providers from different parts of the world were identified through the TAGC (an international group with a listserv of ~250 participants and a board of directors representing GC trainers from 28 countries), the HUGO genetic counseling education subcommittee, the European Board of Medical Genetics, the Genetic Counseling Education Association (representing program leadership of 61 GC programs in North America), and authors of recent publications identified in our literature review. Interested participants completed an online demographic and eligibility survey on REDCap (Research Electronic Data Capture, a secure web application for the management of online surveys). The participant information sheet and consent form were included in the REDCap survey. Where more than four potential participants from the same country expressed interest, participants were selected for breadth of experience (for example, educational pedagogy training and membership of an organization for GC program directors or credentialing/accreditation). Two email reminders were sent to participants during the period each survey was open. 2.2. Data collection 2.2.1. Measure Survey 1 (See Data S1 ) was novelly created for the first round by the authors (HUGO Genetic Counseling Education sub‐committee, AM, KEO, MP) using a list of 64 GC knowledge and skill items obtained through a review of available training guidelines (ACGC, 2023 ; CBGC, 2006 ; EBMG, 2021 ; HGSA, 2022 ). We organized items around 4 core topic areas: (1) Counseling and communication skills and knowledge ( N = 15), (2) Genetic and genomic knowledge ( n = 14), (3) Medical knowledge ( n = 20), and (4) Genetic counseling healthcare professional skills and knowledge ( n = 15). Survey 1 was piloted with 5 experienced GC education professionals from Australasia, the UK, and the USA and revised prior to circulation. Participants were asked to score each item for relevance using a 5‐point Likert‐type scale, where 1 was not relevant and 5 was critically relevant. The Delphi process typically does not allow participants to elaborate or discuss issues that arise (Goodman, 1987 ; Keeney et al., 2001 ); however, we used free text comments (Snape et al., 2014 ) to allow participants to expand on the reasons for their rankings and suggest any knowledge and skill items that were not listed but they felt should be considered. Survey 2 (See Data S2 ) included 36 GC knowledge and skill items that were identified after analysis of the data from Survey 1. For this survey, we maintained items that demonstrated 80% or higher ratings of “critically relevant, (5)” or “highly relevant, (4)” on the first survey. These items were presented to participants on Survey 2 in a binary manner, asking participants to indicate if they agreed or disagreed that the item should be part of a core competency list, with free‐text boxes to indicate reasons for their rankings. Additionally, we included the eight new items that were suggested in the free text comments in Survey 1, using the original 5‐point Likert scale. All participants who completed Survey 1 received an invitation to complete Survey 2. 2.2.2. Data collection methodology The survey was administered online using REDCap between 20th November 2023 and 28th May 2024. Two online consensus meetings were held on 26 June 2024, 12 h apart to facilitate access to participants globally. We took detailed notes and recorded and audio transcribed the meetings so we could refer to participant discussion during data analysis. 2.2.3. Data analysis We used descriptive statistics to analyze the data after each round. Prior to the start of the study, we determined that consensus would be defined as ≥80% of participants scoring the question within the top 2 (high/critically relevant) or bottom 2 (not/limited relevance) options on the scale. Our ≥80% threshold and our decision to collapse the high/critically relevant categories is in line with Delphi methods, where thresholds for consensus typically range from 70% to 80% (Schifano & Niederberger, 2025 ), and with genetic counseling research using Delphi methods (Eubanks Higgins et al., 2013 ). Continuous variables (the Likert scale) are reported in percentages and as the median and mean score, range, and standard deviation. We used the free text responses to identify additional topics not already covered in round 1 and to inform wording changes and additional knowledge/skills in round 2. Consensus meeting notes were reviewed to inform the final version of the curriculum list. 3. RESULTS We obtained 40 complete and seven partial responses to the initial demographic and eligibility survey. Survey 1 was completed by 23 participants from 16 countries, and 17 participants from 13 countries completed survey 2. All round 1 participants were then invited to an online consensus meeting, which was held as 2 separate meetings to account for all time zones. A total of 12 participants from 11 countries attended one of the consensus meetings, and email feedback was received from seven participants, several of whom were unable to attend a consensus meeting and wished to make final comments after circulation of the final draft curriculum list. Demographics for the participants can be found in Table 1 . TABLE 1. Participant characteristics. Variable name n % Country Australia 2 8.7 Austria 1 4.4 Canada 2 8.7 France 1 4.4 Germany 1 4.4 India 1 4.4 Indonesia 1 4.4 Japan 1 4.4 Malaysia 1 4.4 Philippines 2 8.7 Portugal 1 4.4 Saudi Arabia 1 4.4 South Africa 1 4.4 Sweden 1 4.4 United Kingdom 2 8.7 United States of America 4 17.4 Primary role Medical Geneticist (MD) 3 13.0 Genetic Counselor 19 82.6 Genetics Nurse 0 0 Laboratory Geneticist 1 4.4 Bioethicist 0 0 Non‐genetics Medical Specialist (MD) 0 0 Non‐genetics Nurse 0 0 Years of work 0–4 0 0 5–9 3 13.0 10–14 4 17.4 15–19 5 21.7 20–24 4 17.4 25–29 3 13.0 30–34 3 13.0 35+ 1 4.4 Gender Male 4 17.4 Female 19 82.6 Transgender or non‐binary 0 0 Other 0 0 Prefer not to say 0 0 Which of the following specific to training GENETIC COUNSELORS have you done currently or in the past? *Could select multiple Designed genetic counselor training curriculum (e.g., course or program design) 19 82.6 Teach coursework within a genetic counselor training program 22 95.7 Clinically supervise genetic counselors in training 20 86.7 Supervise genetic counselor student research 21 91.3 Provide academic advisement or formal mentorship to genetic counselor trainees 21 91.3 Evaluate genetic counselor training programs (e.g., Accreditation review) 8 34.8 Evaluate genetic counselor applications for certification or registration 7 30.4 Other Serve on genetic counseling governing bodies and/or committees 2 8.7 Work with genetic counselors 1 4.4 External examiner 1 4.4 Faculty teacher of extra programs 1 4.4 Which of the following types of training about education have you completed in the past? *Could select multiple Curriculum design 13 56.5 Assessment/Evaluation processes 16 69.6 Adult learning principles and approaches 13 56.5 Instructional methods 8 34.8 Teaching skills (e.g., large group lecturing, small group discussion) 16 69.6 Online course design/implementation 10 43.5 Other Exam‐item writing 1 4.4 Open in a new tab In round 1, participants ranked 36 (56%) of a possible 64 topics presented across three main domains (knowledge, skills, and attitudes) as being of critical or high relevance (Table S1 ). The items were grouped into four domains for presentation in round 2: counseling and communication skills and knowledge ( n = 10); genetic and genomic knowledge ( n = 7); medical knowledge ( n = 10); and genetic counseling healthcare professional skills and knowledge ( n = 9). Items ranked below the 80% consensus threshold at round 1 included some laboratory methods (for example, cytogenetics) and some medical knowledge, including specialties such as immunogenetics. Direct‐to‐consumer testing also ranked below the consensus threshold. Free text comments indicated that the ranking of these items reflected the availability of laboratory test types in different countries, regional needs, and healthcare system priorities. This content was included for comment at the consensus meetings. Round 1 participants suggested an additional eight topics for inclusion: social and policy issues; telehealth genetic counseling; group or co‐counseling; communicating genetic/genomic test results; genetic/genomic test result interpretation; knowledge of risk assessment tools/programs; selection and evaluation of genetic/genomic tests/laboratories; ability to critically analyze literature for use in genetic counseling practice. Free text comments were grouped into three areas: the need for flexibility to choose content specific to local (country/region) healthcare environments; the impact of prior training of the student significantly impacts areas of emphasis in GC training; and the ability to critically appraise research for use in practice is important, with mixed views on how to achieve this. Participants in the round 2 survey ranked 35 of a possible 44 (80%) items as being of critical or high relevance. In preparation for the consensus meetings, items were grouped into three tiers, with tier 1 items ranked as critical or high relevance by 94%–100% of participants and tier 2 topics ranked as critical or high relevance by 80% or more participants. Remaining topics were placed in tier 3 (Table S2 ). Guided by the free text comments, instructions to select content appropriate to regional needs were added to the genetic and genomic knowledge and medical knowledge domains. An additional item regarding preparation to acquire knowledge and adapt skills was added to the genetic and genomic knowledge and medical knowledge domains. The 3‐tier structure was presented at the consensus meetings for discussion (Table S2 ). Participants recommended a 2‐tier structure to reduce complexity and made suggestions for altered wording. The final list (Table 2 ) comprises 2 tiers and 4 domains. Tier 1 curriculum items ( N = 25) are considered the minimum and critical components to include in a GC training program, with 94%–100% participant agreement across all items. Tier 2 consists of 11 curriculum items to include based on regional needs and/or as GC training develops. During the consensus meetings and by email review afterwards, participants reviewed the list alongside the definition of genetic counseling (National Society of Genetic Counselors'' Definition Task Force et al., 2006 ) and agreed the final, 2‐tier list would prepare GCs for practice in line with the current definition of genetic counseling. TABLE 2. International genetic counselor curriculum consensus list. Tier 1: Minimum and critical components to include in a genetic counselor training program (25 items) Tier 2: Curriculum items to include based on regional needs and/or as genetic counselor training develops (11 items) Domain Curriculum item Curriculum item Counseling and communication skills and knowledge Counseling techniques: Empathy and active listening More advanced counseling concepts that should be strongly considered for inclusion or taught electively include: grief and bereavement models; family‐based counseling theories; disability awareness Interviewing techniques: Developing rapport, asking questions Agenda setting (Contracting) Giving information (Bio‐psycho‐educational models) Decision‐making models Cultural sensitivity training Ethics/ELSI issues relevant to genetic services (Code of ethics, privacy, discrimination, informed consent) 7 items 1 item Genetic and genomic knowledge Basics of Mendelian inheritance Laboratory basics: Genomic sequencing (WGS/WES) Basics of Non‐Mendelian inheritance (Multifactorial, mitochondrial, imprinting) Basics of human reproduction Make appropriate and accurate genetic risk assessment Embryology/ developmental genetics Laboratory basics: Molecular genetics and basic test result interpretation Programs should include additional testing technology, skills and knowledge relevant to regional needs Laboratory basics that allow the learner to acquire knowledge and adapt skills as new technologies emerge (e.g., understanding foundational concepts of DNA analysis methods) 5 items 4 items Medical knowledge Clinical features and natural history of common genetic disorders Select from additional specialty areas relevant to regional needs. For example: neurogenetics, cardiac genetics, metabolic disorders, infertility genetics preimplantation testing. Select from additional specialty areas as these emerge over time Specialty areas: Prenatal genetics Specialty areas: Pediatric and adult genetic disease Specialty areas: Population based screening (Newborn or Carrier) Specialty areas: Cancer genetics Foundational clinical skills to acquire knowledge for other relevant specialty areas now and in the future 6 items 1 item Genetic counseling healthcare professional skills and knowledge Obtain and draw a family history Discuss clinical surveillance and/or clinical screening Analyze pedigree/perform risk assessment Reflective practice and counseling supervision Ability to communicate genetic principles, genetic/genomic testing options and results Effective working relationships including to function within multidisciplinary teams Familiarity with genetics literature, databases, and computerized tools Preparation for lifelong learning across all domains of knowledge and skills Ability to complete case documentation (chart note, patient letters) Genetic counseling healthcare professional skills and knowledge that should be considered for inclusion or taught electively based on regional needs include: selection and evaluation of genetic/genomic tests/laboratories; knowledge of risk assessment tools/programs; telehealth genetic counseling; group or co‐counseling; social and policy issues (including systems change) Recognize and maintain professional boundaries and limitations of own scope of practice Ability to critically analyze literature for use in genetic counseling practice 7 items 5 items Open in a new tab 4. DISCUSSION Challenges while setting up an educational pathway for GCs have been reported across the globe (Baty & Davis, 2023 ; Paneque et al., 2024 ; Sahhar et al., 2013 ). Given the rapid global growth in demand for genetic counseling education and training, along with ongoing challenges in defining the role of a GC, it was timely to review existing curriculum lists and identify areas of consensus on core knowledge and skills. In the current study, we identified a core set of knowledge and skills that should be considered when establishing training and accreditation guidelines in areas where the profession of GC is new or emerging. It is crucial to think about competency in a nuanced way—particularly when defining the purpose of GC training programs. This study identified core knowledge and skills required for students to achieve readiness for practice for entry‐level roles in regions where the profession of GCs is desired or in development. While direct patient care roles are expected to be the focus in locations where the GC profession is emerging (and therefore, we focused on knowledge and skills in these areas for the current study), as the profession evolves, the role of GCs extends into more diverse roles in interdisciplinary settings. Training programs will need to be flexible to prepare GCs to be versatile professionals, capable of working in settings beyond the traditional clinical environments. Building in versatility will enhance the profession''s ability to address evolving needs across health systems, research, laboratories, and public policy. The final list comprises 25 items considered essential for all GC training programs and a further 10 items for consideration based on regional needs and the stage of development of the GC profession and training programs. Most items that would become the tier 1 list were clear from round one. Core counseling and communication knowledge and skills, including counseling techniques, models of decision‐making, ethics issues, and cultural sensitivity, are included in tier 1, while advanced counseling concepts are in tier 2. Genetic and genomic knowledge that prepares GCs to make accurate risk assessments and adapt skills as new technology emerges are tier 1 items, while testing technologies relevant to regional needs are a tier 2 item. Medical knowledge includes core specialty areas such as prenatal, pediatric, and adult genetic disease and foundational clinical skills to acquire knowledge for relevant specialty areas in tier 1. Professional skills and knowledge include familiarity with genetic literature, databases, and computerized tools, case documentation, and critical analysis of literature in tier 1, while reflective practice and a range of skills including co‐counseling and telehealth are in tier 2. The outcome of the consensus process we have undertaken is a flexible curriculum list designed to be adapted to regional contexts. In seeking to identify a core set of knowledge and skills, it was inevitable that items some participants viewed as critically or highly relevant would not be included in the final list. For some items, there was strong agreement that they were not core for inclusion, and for others, despite the scoring, there were rich discussions about how to frame the disagreements. For example, research methods and the requirement to complete an independent research project were removed at round one, and while critical appraisal of the literature eventually gained consensus as a tier 1 item, this only occurred after robust discussion. The final list is focused on practical knowledge and skills, with theoretical models including genetic counseling models and theories of behavior change being removed at round 1. Many of the discussions were about what aspects of medical genetics or genetic testing to include. Often, these discussions led to a re‐framing centered around practical skills or flexibility based on regional needs. For example, population and quantitative genetics and Bayes theorem were removed at round 1 in favor of practical skills around risk assessment. Similarly, variant curation was removed at round one, but following discussion at the consensus meetings, there was agreement to include test interpretation as a tier 1 item. Emerging areas such as polygenic risk scores were removed at round one, perhaps reflecting access, which is predominantly in the research setting. Similarly, the inclusion of cytogenetics was requested by participants in regions where cytogenetic testing is commonly used. The ability to adapt content to regional needs is captured through the inclusion of the item that states programs should include additional testing technology, skills, and knowledge relevant to regional needs and the inclusion of cytogenetics as an example in the instructions for using the curriculum list. Reasons for regional adaptation according to healthcare priorities, regional needs, or available genetic tests were shared by participants while scoring those items. Reflective practice as a skill did not reach consensus for being included in the core list, despite having 80% of participants consider it critically or highly relevant; it was included as a tier 2 item. Nevertheless, it maps to competency standards for GCs in Europe and Australasia, where it is operationalized through access to career‐long reflective practice supervision being included in certification guidelines (Clarke et al., 2007 ; Guimarães et al., 2024 ; McEwen et al., 2025 ; Paneque et al., 2016 , 2023 ). Clinical supervision during training develops students'' ability to reflect on their practice, recognizing areas requiring development as well as areas of competence. Reflective practice and supervision contribute to professional identity formation and play an important role in facilitating professional identity. Differences in the implementation of genetic counseling supervision worldwide have reinforced the need for standardized supervision guidelines (Paneque et al., 2023 ). Master of Genetic Counseling programs play a key role in establishing this critical skill (Costa et al., 2025 ). Thus, we believe reflective practice and supervision should be strongly considered for inclusion in the curriculum. Regions where the profession is emerging are urged to include reflective practice in regulation and credentialing processes to promote global awareness of the relevance of this skill for continuing professional development. Other professions including psychology, counseling, and social work, embrace supervision as a process of reflection and learning for practitioners throughout their careers, and offer established models that GCs can draw on to develop supervision practices (Davys & Beddoe, 2021 ). Other international strategies for promoting collaboration between countries with asymmetric development of the GC profession, and specifically mentoring and training programs for supervisors, may facilitate the implementation of supervision routines and its inherent reflective practice. Implementing curricula content for training future GCs is not without significant challenges, including both pedagogical perspectives and the specific needs that professionals in training may have. Despite the establishment of requirements regarding the expected competencies in regions where the profession is well established, there is limited evidence on how the teaching methods and didactic options are adopted by training programs for GCs. A recent study by members of the Genetic Counseling Education Association examined the evaluation and adaptation of the didactic curriculum within genetic counseling programs in the United States (Vercruyssen et al., 2025 ). They found that the expanding scope of professional knowledge and responsibility for GCs was a challenge, while small program size limits the ability to assess the effectiveness of curriculum changes. An additional challenge in locations where genetic counselors are emerging is the ability to provide clinical supervision from experienced genetic counselors (Paneque et al., 2024 ). The use of simulation with standardized clients and virtual clinical placement experiences has been found to be an effective way to expand access to clinical training, with students reporting satisfaction with simulated learning experiences (Jacobs & McEwen, 2021 , 2025 ). Further efforts to strengthen the evidence base for pedagogical approaches to GC education are needed, particularly as more training programs are introduced. Furthermore, the knowledge and skills required for the training of new GCs identified here as critical for new educational programs must also be adapted to the professional background of each student. This has been a challenge shared by emerging programs and is a key factor for the heterogeneity described in training programs internationally (Ingvoldstad et al., 2016 ). Studies such as ours demonstrate the invaluable role of organizations such as TAGC, HUGO, and many other professional boards in facilitating connections and discussion for GC educators. These organizations can play a key role in developing and promoting guidelines to support academic and governmental institutions in designing curriculum, proposing professional profiles at graduation (entry‐level), and standardizing competency‐based regulation. However, while organizations like TAGC and HUGO play critical international roles in facilitating discussions and creating guidelines, there is a gap in how these guidelines are supported by empirical research. To build a more robust evidence base for global GC education requires financial investment and targeted funding to explore different pedagogical models, their effectiveness, and the way models can be integrated into educational frameworks. As we may not be aware of all established genetic counseling competencies or accreditation requirements, we encourage groups that have such guidance to evaluate and compare their documents to our suggested core list. Further research could also evaluate how program directors in emerging areas retrospectively feel this list matches their own starting curriculums and/or how they think the list would have structured the professional development in their own countries. Having established programs reflect on these core knowledge and skills can also help genetic counseling educators and trainees focus at a time when learning requirements continue to increase globally. 4.1. Limitations We suspect that the length and complexity of the Delphi process, including two surveys and an online meeting, likely contributed to participation drop‐off within our study. In addition, while we attempted to recruit very widely for global representation, we were unable to achieve representation from all the regions that we know are pursuing GC training around the globe. One possibility is that in these regions, where English is not the mother tongue and where they may have less experience developing GC curriculum, participants were less comfortable with participation. The curriculum list reflects the predominantly western definition of genetic counseling, requiring regions to adjust content to reflect their own cultural and healthcare contexts. 5. CONCLUSION In response to the global demand for genetic counselor education and training and using a consensus process with GC educators from across the globe, we have agreed upon a core list of knowledge and skills to guide GC education providers. The list retains flexibility to address the professional backgrounds of students entering programs and regional differences in technology availability and health system preferences. As the GC profession continues to grow and evolve, we urge educators to connect through global organizations such as HUGO and TAGC to share resources and support the development of quality, accredited education and training programs. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS AM, KEO, and MP contributed substantially to the conception of the work, development of the measures, and analysis of the data, drafted and revised the manuscript, approved the final version, and agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work. YC‐K contributed to survey construction and data analysis and approved the final version of the manuscript. The HUGO Genetic Counseling Education Sub‐Committee contributed to the development of the original list of curricula items, circulated calls for recruitment, and ensured member regions were represented. FUNDING INFORMATION No external funding was obtained. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT Several of the authors are members of the HUGO Genetic Counseling Subcommittee (AM, KEO, MP), and KEO is the current President of the Transnational Alliance for Genetic Counseling (TAGC). ETHICS STATEMENT All study methods and measures underwent Human Research Ethics Committee review by University of Technology Sydney (UTS), review number ETH23‐8497. PATIENT CONSENT STATEMENT Participants provided written consent before participation. PERMISSION TO REPRODUCE MATERIAL FROM OTHER SOURCES Not applicable. CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION Not applicable. Supporting information Table S1. JGC4-34-0-s002.xlsx (190.7KB, xlsx) Table S2. JGC4-34-0-s001.docx (548.9KB, docx) Data S1. JGC4-34-0-s003.docx (423.6KB, docx) Data S2. JGC4-34-0-s004.docx (420.1KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors thank all Delphi participants for their thoughtful contributions to the work and the professional organizations that circulated the call for recruitment. Open access publishing facilitated by University of Technology Sydney, as part of the Wiley ‐ University of Technology Sydney agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. McEwen, A. , Ormond, K. E. , Cathcart‐King, Y. ; Paneque, M. (2025). Developing global consensus about core knowledge and skills for genetic counselor education. Journal of Genetic Counseling, 34, e70116. 10.1002/jgc4.70116 Alison McEwen, Kelly E. Ormond and Milena Paneque contributed equally to this article. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT We have included all relevant data in the supplemental tables of this publication. REFERENCES Accreditation Council for Genetic Counseling (ACGC) . (2023). Practice based comptencies for genetic counselors . https://www.nsgc.org/Portals/0/Docs/Education/ACGC‐Core‐Competencies‐Brochure_15_Web.pdf Ambrosino, E. , Abou Tayoun, A. N. , Abramowicz, M. , Zilfalil, B. A. , Boughtwood, T. , Hamdi, Y. , Hubbard, T. , Kato, K. , Lopes‐Cendes, I. , Majumder, P. P. , Mascalzoni, D. , Ndiaye, R. , Ramsay, M. , Repetto, G. M. , Shotelersuk, V. , Taylor, S. , Reeder, J. C. , & Ross, A. L. (2024). The WHO genomics program of work for equitable implementation of human genomics for global health. Nature Medicine, 30(10), 2711–2713. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ashfaq, M. , Ahmed, S. A. , Aziz‐Rizvi, R. , Hasan, Z. , Kirmani, S. , Munim, S. , Naeem, R. , Raza, J. , & Furqan, A. (2023). Identifying the current status and future needs of clinical, educational, and laboratory genetics services in Pakistan: A web‐based panel discussion. Journal of Community Genetics, 14(1), 71–80. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Austin, J. (2024). Unlocking the next phase of development for our profession: Developing and consolidating the recognit